\f2 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f0\b\fs36 Version 1.0: The Original Catastrophe\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f1 Johnny's first experiment featured what could generously be called a "vertical lathe," though calling it that was rather like calling a rabid badger a "domestic pet" \'96 technically accurate but missing several crucial nuances about the danger involved.\
The apparatus consisted of a single Gyges Disc mounted horizontally on a high-speed spindle, rather like a record player designed by someone who had heard records described but never actually seen one, and who harbored deep suspicions about the structural integrity of reality. The disc was held in place by what Johnny optimistically termed "precision clamps" but which more closely resembled the kind of vice grips one might use to remove a particularly stubborn bolt from a spacecraft that was actively exploding.\
Control was achieved through a simple interface that featured a large red button labeled "GO" and a smaller, less prominent button labeled "STOP" \'96 the sort of design philosophy that suggested Johnny's engineers had learned their craft from watching too many cartoons. The speed could be adjusted via a dial that went from "Gentle Rotation" to "Ludicrous Speed," with several intermediate settings whose labels had been thoughtfully obscured by scorch marks from previous experiments.\
When activated, the disc would spin with increasing velocity while emitting what observers described as "an ominous humming sound," which was the universe's way of politely suggesting that perhaps this wasn't such a good idea. The testing chamber was surrounded by what Johnny called "safety glass," though its ability to contain the forces being generated was roughly equivalent to using a paper bag to hold a particularly energetic supernova.\
The experiment's conclusion \'96 involving blue lightning, destructive radiation, and the violent deaths of several technicians \'96 had taught Johnny one valuable lesson: that single discs were apparently temperamental creatures that required more sophisticated handling.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f0\b\fs36 Version 2.0: The Binary Configuration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f1 Project Kebab's second iteration represented what Johnny considered a triumph of engineering sophistication, which was rather like considering a particularly elaborate mousetrap a triumph of aerospace engineering. Learning from his previous experience (specifically, the part where everything exploded), Johnny had developed a dual-disc system based on the Sages' cryptic advice about "binary configurations" and "cosmic energy exchanges."\
The new apparatus featured two Gyges Discs mounted on separate spindles, positioned precisely three meters apart and oriented to face each other like cosmic dance partners preparing for a waltz that might accidentally tear holes in space-time. Each disc was held by improved "containment assemblies" \'96 essentially the same vice grips as before, but painted a more reassuring shade of blue and equipped with additional warning labels.\
The control system had evolved into what Johnny proudly called a "synchronized dual-interface matrix," which consisted of two sets of the same red and smaller buttons, now connected by an impressive tangle of cables that suggested either advanced engineering or the world's most dangerous Christmas tree lights. A new feature was the "resonance harmonization dial," which allowed operators to adjust the relative speeds of the two discs \'96 a capability that had roughly the same relationship to wisdom as juggling chainsaws had to personal safety.\
When properly calibrated, the twin discs would spin in perfect synchronization, creating what the Sages described as "temporal stability through paradoxical motion" and what the technicians described as "that thing that makes the coffee taste funny and causes spontaneous nosebleeds." The energy exchange between the discs created visible patterns in the air \'96 concentric ripples that seemed to bend light and occasionally made observers question whether they were looking at reality or at reality's evil twin.\
The testing chamber for this version featured "enhanced containment protocols," which meant thicker glass and several additional warning signs, including one that simply read "NO, SERIOUSLY, DON'T TOUCH ANYTHING" in seventeen languages.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f0\b\fs36 Version 3.0: The Pentagram of Doom\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f1 The final configuration represented Johnny's ultimate vision of Gyges Disc mastery, which was roughly equivalent to a small child's ultimate vision of what happens when you connect every firework in the shop to a single, very large fuse.\
Five Gyges Discs were now mounted on individual spindles arranged in a perfect pentagram formation, each disc positioned at the points of a five-pointed star inscribed within a circle exactly seven meters in diameter. The choice of five discs had been inspired by Chilo's cryptic reference to "the power of the light bestowed upon the keepers of time and space in the likeness of a pentagram," which Johnny had interpreted as a sophisticated scientific principle rather than what it actually was: the ramblings of someone who had spent too much time thinking about cosmic forces and not nearly enough time thinking about basic safety protocols.\
Each spindle was now equipped with "quantum-synchronized rotation assemblies" \'96 essentially the same vice grips again, but now painted in five different colors and connected to a central control matrix that looked like the result of a passionate encounter between a telephone switchboard and a particularly ambitious Christmas display. The control system had evolved into what Johnny called the "Pentagonal Resonance Coordination Interface," which featured five sets of red and smaller buttons arranged in a star pattern around a central master control that could synchronize all five discs into what he optimistically termed "harmonic convergence."\
The most sophisticated addition was the "variable resonance modulation system," which allowed operators to adjust not just the speed of individual discs, but their relative phases, creating complex interference patterns that could theoretically stabilize the reality-bending effects. In practice, this was rather like trying to conduct a symphony orchestra where each musician was playing a different piece of music while riding a roller coaster that was on fire.\
When fully activated, the five discs created a spectacular light show of interconnected energy patterns that resembled a cosmic mandala designed by someone with serious issues about the nature of existence. The visual effect was described by observers as "beautiful, terrifying, and probably illegal in several dimensions." The air above the pentagram formation would shimmer with what appeared to be fractal patterns that seemed to contain glimpses of other realities \'96 some better, some worse, and some that raised serious questions about the sanity of whoever was running the universe next door.\
The testing chamber for this final version had been reinforced with what Johnny called "reality-grade containment barriers," which were essentially very thick walls surrounded by equipment that nobody fully understood but which made reassuring humming noises and had lots of blinking lights. Warning signs had been replaced by a simple statement: "If you can read this, you're probably too close."\
The entire apparatus was controlled from a separate observation room connected by what the engineers optimistically called "a safe distance," though given the tendency of Gyges Disc experiments to redefine fundamental concepts like "distance" and "safety," this was perhaps more hope than engineering principle.\
As Johnny prepared for the final test, he reflected that the evolution from one disc to five represented humanity's eternal quest to take something that was already quite dangerous enough and make it five times more so, just to see what would happen. It was, he mused, probably the most human thing he'd ever done.\
}